# TODO - Implement QCA9565 Bluetooth USB support

## Owner directive

The project must implement Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and AirDrop. QCA9565/DW1707 is a
combined Wi-Fi/Bluetooth 4.0 chip, not an assumed separate Bluetooth chipset.
Shared silicon does not replace the Bluetooth USB HCI/firmware driver path.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement source-backed USB identity matching, HCI transport and any
      required firmware bootstrap for the target QCA9565 Bluetooth function.
      Never infer an exact USB VID/PID from the WLAN PCI ID alone.
- [ ] Research the target Tahoe Bluetooth host/USB contracts and firmware
      ROM/build matching. Use licensed firmware with correct notices; never
      substitute unrelated firmware or advertise readiness without startup.
- [ ] Integrate Bluetooth start/stop, reset, ownership, error reporting,
      sleep/resume and Wi-Fi/Bluetooth coexistence in the production target.
- [ ] Connect BLE discovery/advertising and the macOS Bluetooth stack needed
      by the required AirDrop implementation; no fabricated adapter/state.
- [ ] Verify protocol behavior with offline source-backed fixtures and record
      remaining platform assumptions. No physical log, EEPROM dump or pairing
      test is a prerequisite for completing this source implementation.

## Evidence

Qualcomm describes QCA9565 as a combined WLAN/Bluetooth 4 solution; Dell G66T8
provides both driver functions and lists Inspiron 5567 support:
https://www.qualcomm.com/networking-infrastructure/products/qca9565
https://www.dell.com/support/home/en-us/drivers/driversdetails?driverid=g66t8

Linux's independent Bluetooth USB/firmware implementations are useful source
references, not macOS implementations or evidence of the notebook's USB ID:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/drivers/bluetooth/ath3k.c
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c

## Local source references

The retained `src/linux/` reference tree already contains `ath3k.c`,
`btusb_main.c`, `btusb_qcom.c/.h`, `btqca.c/.h`, HCI headers, HCI event/core
handlers and LE advertising/EIR handling. Do not duplicate or replace these
files merely to reacquire them. Atheros USB bootstrap and the generic HCI
transport must both be integrated; PCI WLAN startup does not perform them.

Missing BLE security references `net/bluetooth/smp.c`, `ecdh_helper.c` and
`ecdh_helper.h` were downloaded from Linux v6.12, resolved to commit
`adc218676eef25575469234709c2d87185ca223a`. Their exact hashes,
upstream URLs, licensing
and reference-only scope are recorded in `src/linux/linux-port-ledger.jsonl`.
The local reference tree remains intentionally excluded from Git. These Linux
files are research input, not linked macOS code or a completed Bluetooth path.

## Implementation evidence as of 2026-11-10

- Commit `830bc1f` corrects pending BLE LE scan HCI Command Status handling:
  command rejection, missing command credits, successful preliminary status
  without premature state promotion, and unrelated-opcode isolation. Offline
  C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan and direct strict Clang-Tidy fixtures passed.
- Commit `ab03625` adds `src/bluetooth/LeAdvertising.{hpp,cpp}` to the **normal
  production Xcode source target**. It emits actual Bluetooth HCI LE Set
  Advertising Parameters (`0x2006`), Set Advertising Data (`0x2008`) and
  Set Advertising Enable (`0x200a`) command packets through the existing
  USB HCI callback transport, rather than fabricating an advertising state.
  It uses legacy non-connectable undirected advertising (`ADV_NONCONN_IND`),
  validates bounded AD structures, takes a private data snapshot, and
  requires an explicit native owner authorization callback for the payload,
  controller procedure, public-address/privacy policy and interval.
  It checks controller Command Complete/Status outcomes, handles disable
  confirmation, command-credit loss, canceled setup, rejected operations,
  stale epoch and unexpected transport loss. The new standalone synthetic
  USB HCI fixture passed C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, direct strict Clang-Tidy,
  format and Darwin C++14 SDK syntax checks. These are **offline** checks,
  not physical advertising tests or a Bluetooth stack integration proof.
- Commit `a7406b6` protects the ATH3K firmware USB owner boundary: after
  each inter-chunk delay it rechecks ownership before submitting bulk data,
  and reports `IdentityLost` for owner revocation during state/version reads.
  A synthetic USB callback test covers all three transitions; C++14/C++17
  ASan+UBSan, focused strict Clang-Tidy and formatting passed. The latest
  complete repository check had 534 passes, 36 existing strict Clang-Tidy
  failures and zero skips; these are source-only results, not USB operation.
- Commit `3437aed` validates USB epoch ownership immediately after each
  borrowed firmware resource lookup, before parsing the patch trailer or
  submitting transfers. It reports `IdentityLost` when a resource callback
  succeeds or fails while revoking ownership. Synthetic C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan,
  direct strict Clang-Tidy and formatting checks passed; no native firmware
  distribution or USB execution is implied.
- Commit `98a502a` bounds native HCI Event/ACL USB IN polls to the
  existing finite transport timeout, so a silent interrupt endpoint cannot
  block the USB service indefinitely. Zero-byte timeouts produce a distinct
  nonfaulting `UsbHciStatus::Idle`; post-I/O lease checks reject an epoch
  revoked while the physical transfer was pending. Partial/ambiguous I/O
  still faults the controller transport. Darwin C++14 SDK syntax, focused
  strict Clang-Tidy and formatting passed. No live USB polling was tested.
- Commit `582c46a` factors the native IN-transfer outcome policy into the
  portable `classifyUsbHciRead` helper and exercises 12 synthetic outcomes,
  including successful short/full reads, idle timeout, partial transfer,
  empty successful transfer, oversize, contradictory status and stale owner.
  C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, focused strict Clang-Tidy, formatting and native
  Darwin C++14 syntax passed. The full repository validator was blocked by
  concurrent validator runs, so its post-change aggregate is **not yet
  verified**. These source checks do not establish macOS HCI host attachment,
  USB lifetime safety at detach, real controller readiness, or AirDrop.
- Commit `69cd43c` requires a controller-confirmed live connection handle
  for both classic and LE ACL transmission and delivery. Buffer-size
  negotiation alone no longer grants authority to host-chosen handles;
  disconnected and retired handles cannot consume packet credits or
  deliver incoming bulk ACL data. Synthetic regressions exercise classic
  connection events, handle-slot reuse, pre-connection RX denial and
  post-disconnection RX rejection. C++14/C++17 offline ASan+UBSan,
  direct strict Clang-Tidy and scoped formatting passed. This does not
  establish native macOS Bluetooth host registration, HCI traffic, or
  physical controller behavior.
- Commit feccb08 adds host-authorized **active** legacy LE scanning
  alongside existing passive scanning. The portable controller now sends
  actual HCI LE Set Scan Parameters (0x200b) with scan type 1 only after
  an explicit Bluetooth-host approval of exclusive procedure ownership,
  public-address privacy policy and scan timing. It rechecks the live USB
  epoch after authorization, tracks active versus passive sessions, and
  suppresses unsolicited SCAN_RSP reports on passive scans. Synthetic
  regressions cover denied/revoked authorization, command contents,
  controller completion/cancellation and active/passive report dispatch.
  C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan, scoped strict Clang-Tidy and formatting passed.
  This does **not** attach the real macOS host or establish AirDrop readiness.
- Commit `47d4546` validates any needed ATH3K ROM-clock configuration
  filename before uploading the patch. Unsupported ROM clocks now fail with
  `UnsupportedClock` and **no** prior firmware writes or forced USB reset;
  already-loaded configurations do not require a ROM clock mapping.
  Synthetic C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan and scoped strict Clang-Tidy passed.
- Commit `72aff64` closes the USB HCI transport's direct-attach admission
  gap: even a caller bypassing the descriptor selector must present a nonzero
  physical owner epoch and distinct interrupt/ACL IN pipe addresses. Failure
  leaves the adapter detached. C++14/C++17 sanitizer and direct Tidy
  regressions passed.
- Commit `3f6e72c` keeps a successful preliminary HCI Command Status
  pending until the matching Command Complete for all five implemented LE
  advertising/scanning setup opcodes. Duplicate preliminary statuses and
  overtaking HCI commands are rejected, while final controller rejections
  remain terminal. LE advertising/discovery host fixtures now account
  preliminary status and completion through the **same** USB HCI event path,
  not just the producer state machine. All three Bluetooth fixtures passed
  C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan; scoped strict Clang-Tidy and formatting passed.
  This does not attach macOS's Bluetooth host or prove actual HCI traffic.
- The last completed whole-repository validation on the integrated
  `362d385` snapshot (before `3f6e72c`) reported **576 checks, 542 passed,
  34 pre-existing strict Clang-Tidy failures, zero skipped checks and zero
  source-snapshot errors**. The latest LE-only source changes have focused
  positive checks, not an independently completed full-tree validator.
- The LE advertising and scanning state machines now handle HCI controller
  responses dispatched *inside* the USB control-OUT callback. Reentrant
  Command Status rejection cannot leave the outer begin, enable or disable
  operation reporting `Ok` after its nested producer has faulted. A valid
  inline disable completion can clear the session epoch without causing a
  spurious `OwnershipLost` result. The C++14/C++17 offline fixtures dispatch
  these synthetic controller responses through `receiveUsbHciEvent` and
  the same LE event handlers, including rejection, successful setup and
  synchronous disable. Scoped sanitizer, formatting and strict Clang-Tidy
  checks passed; this is not an actual USB/Bluetooth host integration.
- Remaining required source: bind the actually running macOS Bluetooth host
  and USB interface to a single authorized owner of command/event traffic,
  wire advertising and discovery to that host and the genuine AirDrop BLE
  policy/content, obtain matching licensed ATH3K firmware and independently
  verified Bluetooth function USB identity, and implement RF coexistence and
  reset/sleep recovery. The portable advertising producer is **not invoked by
  a native Bluetooth host yet**. The acceptance checklist stays open.

- The LE legacy scan event dispatcher now stops publishing the remainder of a
  multi-report HCI advertising event when its first report observer cancels
  the scan or revokes the state synchronously. A valid in-callback cancellation
  keeps the pending controller disable and a deliberate revoke stays Idle; an
  independently revoked owner during active scanning still faults closed. The
  synthetic two-report fixture reproduces the old canceled-delivery bug
  (unpatched source exits 105) and covers synchronous stop and revoke.
  Both modified translation units passed direct strict Clang-Tidy/format;
  C++14 and C++17 host runs passed ASan+UBSan. This changes only portable HCI
  consumer behavior, not the missing native Bluetooth host or AirDrop linkage.

- The USB HCI transport now checks its attached physical owner epoch after
  every borrowed command-OUT, ACL-OUT and completed packet-delivery callback.
  A callback that synchronously detaches and attaches a replacement session
  reports `OwnershipLost` without modifying the new session's fault, packet
  credit or command accounting; deliberate detach alone remains detached.
  The synthetic `testStaleHciEpochCallbacks` covers all three callback types,
  both callback results and both detach/rebind outcomes (12 cases). The
  regression exits 14 with unmodified transport source, while the corrected
  C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan host fixtures and direct strict Clang-Tidy and
  formatting checks pass. This does not establish native Bluetooth host
  registration, a real USB re-enumeration or device readiness.
